{
  "passage": "The Cholas were a powerful dynasty that ruled from the Kaveri delta in present-day Tamil Nadu. Their power grew greatly under Rajaraja I, who became king in 985 CE, and his son Rajendra I. Rajaraja built a strong army and navy and extended Chola rule to Sri Lanka and the Maldives. Rajendra I went further. His armies marched to the Ganga in the north, and his navy attacked kingdoms in Southeast Asia. To celebrate his victory in the north, he built a new capital called Gangaikondacholapuram. The Cholas are especially remembered for their temples. The Brihadeeswara temple at Thanjavur, built by Rajaraja I and completed in about 1010 CE, is one of the largest temples in India. Its tower rises about 66 metres high. Chola temples were not only places of worship. They were centres of town life, with land, schools, music and dance attached to them, and they gave work to many craftspeople. Chola artists were also famous for their bronze statues, made by a method called lost-wax casting. In this method, a figure is first made of wax and covered with clay. When the clay is heated, the wax melts and flows out, and molten metal is poured into the hollow mould. The bronze figures of Nataraja, the dancing Shiva, are among the finest examples.",
